Z||000007||jch82||08-06-2001||01:42 PM||||About the same thing is happening to me. I have 130,000mi on my 2.8 and it has a little knock but it stops as soon as the tach hits 2500rpm. I wish I could pinpoint the problem but I will most likely be rebuilding this winter...<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mom and Dad they quite don't understand it<BR>ALl the kids they laugh as if they planned it<BR>Why do girls wanna pierce their nose <BR>And walk around in torn pantyhose, oh yeah<BR>                 <I>-Blink 182</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Joe Torma||08-06-2001||02:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jch82:<BR><B>About the same thing is happening to me. I have 130,000mi on my 2.8 and it has a little knock but it stops as soon as the tach hits 2500rpm. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Hows your oil pressure when hot?  Your problem "sounds" more like a top end issue.<BR>Sloppy timing chains make some noise too.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||86SE||08-06-2001||02:40 PM||||Is it possible for me to replace all rod bearings with the motor still in the car, ie. raise the car up, take off oil pan, and work from there?<P>How much life does the lil ol 2.8 that could have left?<P>Chuck<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000010||86SE||08-07-2001||01:29 AM||||Checked oil, quart low.  Filled it and the knock has been quieted when the engine is hot.  When I first start the engine you cant hear the knock.  But the knock could be a rattle because once the engine gets above 3 grand there is no 'knock'.  But this whole knock thing is good, that means i have to get the 3.4L project underway ASAP!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Just wonderin how far this motor will get me.  I would like about 5,000 miles, heck, 5,001 miles out of this motor yet.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||8
Z||000011||artherd||08-07-2001||01:48 AM||||Ah, gotta love ()@#&$*(#@$&(8 GM!!!!<P>They always pick the best time to go out too, just when you can't afford either the time, OR money to fix. :(<P>Ditch the 2.8 faster than you can say "what crappy pushrod"   the 3.4 is a more reliable (not to mention powerful) motor, personally I'm going straight to a Northstar which is a rarity, a reliable General Motors engine!<P>The Fiero really deserves better than it got, I wish you teh best of luck in your (unsuspended?) 3.4 project :)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||mindscape||08-07-2001||01:55 AM||||i <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 86SE:<BR><B>Is it possible for me to replace all rod bearings with the motor still in the car, ie. raise the car up, take off oil pan, and work from there?<P>How much life does the lil ol 2.8 that could have left?<P>Chuck<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes.<P>Sometime in 1998 I installed a high volume oil pump and new rods and mains.  That was at about 135K miles; crank was slightly streaked - minor polishing while in car (fun!).  Recently the engine died, worn cam lobe - bad cam gear - galled lifter, but I drove it to 190K+ miles.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Joe Torma||08-07-2001||09:46 AM||||Yes its possible to replace when in the car.  I've done it a couple of times.  Glad adding the oil worked! Your lifters were probably starving! Noises and stuff can be hundreds of things!  Could be a sloppy piston too...without hearing it who knows!<P>If you replace the bearings, make sure the back sides are COMPLETELY free of oil.  You don't want to spin a bearing.<P>Joe<P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||enslaved||08-07-2001||12:22 PM||||Before you go and do any major work on your engine, it could very well be a spark knock. Try some high octane gas for the next 2 or 3 fillups. If that doesn't do it, then look into other problems. But i had this same problem with mine. And the high octane fixed it.||||reg||1
